\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Reflection positivity for retained-half paths}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{G} be compact metrizable. Suppose the fine and coarse
graphs of a \vocabk{coarse path map}{fdip-00MO} have
involutive graph reflections. Reflected edges are interpreted
as oriented traversals, with inversion when orientation is
reversed. Suppose each chosen reflected coarse path is the
reflected fine path, in its traversal order. Also choose
positive-half edge sets #{E_{f,+},E_{c,+}} such that every
path assigned to #{a\in E_{c,+}} uses only edges in
#{E_{f,+}}, in either orientation.}

\p{Let #{\lambda_f} be a reflection-invariant probability
whose reflected pairing is nonnegative on the specified
algebra #{\mathcal A_{f,+}} of bounded positive-half
observables. Let #{\mathcal A_{c,+}} consist of bounded
coarse observables whose pullbacks belong to that algebra.
Then #{\lambda_c=\mathcal B_*\lambda_f} is reflection
invariant and
##{\int\overline{F(\theta_cu)}F(u)\,d\lambda_c(u)\ge0
 \qquad(F\in\mathcal A_{c,+}).}
If the fine algebra contains all bounded functions of
#{E_{f,+}}, this includes every bounded function of
#{E_{c,+}}. If the fine algebra is restricted to gauge-invariant
observables, impose the same restriction on #{F}.}

\proof{
\p{Reflecting each oriented traversal and then multiplying
in the original order gives
#{\mathcal B\theta_f=\theta_c\mathcal B}. By the path
support hypothesis, a retained positive-half observable
depends only on the permitted fine half after pullback.
Gauge-invariant coarse functions have gauge-invariant
pullbacks by \ref{fdip-00MP}. Thus the hypotheses of the
general pushforward theorem \ref{fdip-00G8} hold, and it
gives the displayed inequality. Reflection invariance
follows separately by equivariance and pushforward of
#{\theta_{f*}\lambda_f=\lambda_f}.}
}

\p{A path crossing outside the specified fine positive
half does not meet the support hypothesis. Its exact image
law still exists by \ref{fdip-00MQ}, but that measure identity
alone gives no reflection-positivity conclusion for the
coarse positive-half algebra.}
